<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Llangadwaladr</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Llangadwaladr is a small village in south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglesey">Anglesey</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, near the royal court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberffraw">Aberffraw</a>. Its church, dedicated to Cadwaladr, holds the Cadfan Stone, a seventh-century inscribed memorial in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> to Cadfan ap Iago, king of Gwynedd, describing him as the wisest and most renowned of all kings. It is one of the earliest monuments of the kings of Gwynedd.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/llangadwaladr.jpg" alt="Llangadwaladr" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eric Jones · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eglwys_St_Cadwaladr_Church.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
